Details: The failing test 'skipUsingRead' wraps an InputStream with a FilterInputStream that overrides skip(long) to always return 0, simulating a stream whose skip method cannot skip any bytes. The IOUtils.skip method under test relies on InputStream.skip, looping and calling skip repeatedly until the requested number of bytes have been skipped or skip returns 0. When skip returns 0, IOUtils.skip breaks out of the loop, assuming EOF. However, the javadoc for IOUtils.skip promises that it only stops early on EOF, but many InputStream implementations (such as CipherInputStream or this test's FilterInputStream) can return 0 from skip even if not at EOF. This leads to IOUtils.skip skipping fewer bytes than requested, violating its contract. The bug is thus caused by IOUtils.skip's premature break when skip returns 0 without attempting any alternative skipping strategy (such as reading and discarding bytes). The recent bug reports do not address this input-stream skipping behavior, confirming that the root cause is in how IOUtils.skip processes InputStream.skip's results.

Best location to fix:
org.apache.commons.compress.utils.IOUtils.skip(java.io.InputStream,long)

